{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-145626",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14177883/assets/7790a013e37bb86c00b44b1c022d6597f9bbdd57616c48e7ee7dfe8ddf512c63.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14177883/assets/379f0690fdbe92b7fb8f66cce4f12b214a23c6299fb8df71edf56d37fc0387fe.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(9,10)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,10)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14177883/assets/90a6730747b59a9886acbdf21880ca6a7ec3266142522ea5b32f07090963a98d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X\\le 9,Y\\le 10) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(9,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 9,Y\\le 10)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,10)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 10)}} \\\\  & =F(9,10)-F(3,10) \\\\  & =0.65-0.12 \\\\  & =0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
